// result_end

\caption(Left: the predicted class of one stump (black) and one fully grown tree (grey), drawn as 0 for *a* and 1 for *b*, and the vote share for *b* of 500 bootstrapped stumps (blue) and 500 bootstrapped deep trees (red). The ten training points are drawn at 0 (*a*) and 1 (*b*). Right: how often each midpoint was chosen as a stump's threshold.)
\alttext(Left: step curves over x from 0.5 to 10.5. The single stump jumps from 0 to 1 at x equals 3.5; the single deep tree jumps up and down at every change of class, dropping to 0 around x equals 5 and 8. The 500-stump vote share rises in small steps from 0.02 to 0.94; the 500-deep-tree share rises more steeply but dips to about 0.3 at x equals 5 and 0.35 at x equals 8. Black dots at height 0 mark the a points at 1, 2, 3, 5 and 8; red dots at height 1 the b points. Right: a bar chart of stump thresholds, tallest at 3.5 (0.39) and 5.5 (0.26), then 8.5 (0.10), with small bars elsewhere and none at 1.5.)

The single stump fitted to all ten points splits at 3.5 and gets 8 of 10 right. The bootstrap
moves that threshold: 38.6% of the 500 stumps split after \(x=3\) and 25.6% after \(x=5\), the two
cuts that separate the most points, and the rest spread over the other gaps. The vote share of
the stumps therefore rises in steps, from 0.02 at \(x\le2\) through 0.41 at \(x=4\) to 0.94 at \(x=10\): it
reads like a smoothed probability of class *b*. The deep trees each fit their own sample
exactly, so their vote follows the data more closely: 0.30 at the *a* at \(x=5\) and 0.35 at the *a*
at 8, where each deep tree that drew the point predicts *a* there. Fitting the data more closely
is not better here. The out-of-bag accuracy is 0.600 for the stumps and 0.500 for the deep trees,
whose isolated *a*'s at 5 and 8 are exactly what a held-out vote gets wrong.

>> A noisy disc in the plane

A two-dimensional example shows the variance at work. The points are uniform on the square
\([-1,1]^2\), class 1 inside the disc of radius \(\sqrt{2/\pi}=0.798\) that fills half the square, class 2
outside, and 15% of the labels flipped (`dm::datasets::circle`). The Bayes rule is the disc
itself, with error 0.15. We fit 200 points and measure the error on 20000 fresh ones.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rf_circle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto train = dm::datasets::circle(200, 2, 0.15, 1), test = dm::datasets::circle(20000, 2, 0.15, 2);
  const auto truth = test.get_column<std::string>("class").values();
  struct Model {
    std::string name;
    dm::stats::RandomForestClassifierOptions options;
  };
  const std::vector<Model> models{{"one tree (all rows, m = 2)", {.n_trees = 1, .max_features = 2, .bootstrap = false}},
                                  {"bagging (500 trees, m = 2)", {.n_trees = 500, .max_features = 2}},
                                  {"random forest (500 trees, m = 1)", {.n_trees = 500, .max_features = 1}}};
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::vector<double> test_error, train_error;
  std::vector<std::optional<double>> oob_error;  // none for the unbootstrapped tree
  std::vector<dm::graphics::RPlot> plots;
  for (const auto& m : models) {
    const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(train, "class ~ x1 + x2", m.options);
    const auto predicted = forest.predict(test);
    double wrong = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < truth.size(); ++i) wrong += predicted[i] != truth[i];
    names.push_back(m.name);
    test_error.push_back(wrong / truth.size());
    train_error.push_back(1.0 - forest.training_accuracy());
    oob_error.push_back(m.options.bootstrap ? std::optional(1.0 - forest.oob_accuracy()) : std::nullopt);
    if (m.options.n_trees == 1 || m.options.max_features == 1) {
      auto p = forest.plot_decision_regions("x1", "x2", 150, std::array<double, 4>{-1.0, 1.0, -1.0, 1.3});
      std::vector<double> cx, cy;
      for (int k = 0; k <= 200; ++k)
        cx.push_back(std::sqrt(2.0 / std::numbers::pi) * std::cos(k * std::numbers::pi / 100)),
            cy.push_back(std::sqrt(2.0 / std::numbers::pi) * std::sin(k * std::numbers::pi / 100));
      p.lines(cx, cy, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5);
      p.title(m.options.n_trees == 1 ? "One tree" : "Random forest, 500 trees");
      plots.push_back(p);
    }
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("model", names);
  table.add_column("training error", train_error);
  table.add_column("out-of-bag error", oob_error);
  table.add_column("test error", test_error);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 3;
  std::print("{}\n\nBayes error: 0.150.\n\n", table.to_md(md));
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (auto& p : plots) fig.add(p);
  fig.size(1100, 520).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/rf_circle.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| model | training error | out-of-bag error | test error |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| one tree (all rows, m = 2) | 0.015 | NA | 0.242 |
| bagging (500 trees, m = 2) | 0.000 | 0.185 | 0.226 |
| random forest (500 trees, m = 1) | 0.015 | 0.170 | 0.212 |


Bayes error: 0.150.

\image(figures/rf_circle.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The class predicted over the square by one tree grown on all 200 points (left) and by a random forest of 500 trees with \(m=1\) (right). Class 1 is blue, class 2 red. The forest's shading is darkest where at least 90% of the trees agree, paler for 60% to 90%, palest below 60%. The black circle is the Bayes boundary; the dots are the 200 training points.)
\alttext(Two maps of the square from minus 1 to 1 with blue and red training points and a black circle of radius 0.8. Left, one tree: solid blue and red rectangles, a blue block roughly filling the circle but with red slivers and notches inside it around red points and blue strips outside it, including thin vertical blue stripes near x1 equals minus 0.85 and 0.8. Right, the forest: dark blue in the middle of the disc, dark red in the corners, and pale or almost white bands along the circle and around isolated points of the wrong colour, where the trees disagree.)

All three models fit their training points almost perfectly (training error 0.015 or 0), and all
are far above the Bayes error on new points. The single tree has test error 0.242. Bagging 500
trees lowers it to 0.226, and choosing one predictor at random per split lowers it further to
0.212. The single tree's map shows why it does badly: rectangles chase individual flipped labels,
leaving red notches inside the disc and blue strips outside it. The forest still makes small
pockets around some flipped points (its trees are grown deep), but the votes average most of
them away, and the pale shading marks where the trees disagree: along the true boundary and
around isolated points of the wrong class. The out-of-bag errors (0.185 and 0.170) are below the
test errors by about 0.04 for this one training set. The simulation below shows that this is
noise in the out-of-bag estimate, not a bias.

> Checking the theory

>> How often a row is out of bag

For several \(n\), a forest of 500 trees on \(n\) rows. The table gives the share of rows each tree
leaves out, against \(q_n\). Splitting the 500 trees into 50 forests of 10, it gives the mean and
variance, over all rows and forests, of the number of trees for which a row is out of bag, against
the binomial \(10q_n\) and \(10q_n(1-q_n)\). Splitting them into 100 forests of 5, it gives the share
of rows never out of bag, against \((1-q_n)^5\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::vector<double> ns, frac, q, mean_count, mean_th, var_count, var_th, never5, never5_th;
  for (const std::size_t n : {10, 20, 50, 150, 1000}) {
    std::vector<double> x(n);
    std::vector<std::string> y(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) x[i] = double(i), y[i] = i % 2 ? "a" : "b";
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
    d.add_column("x", x);
    d.add_column("y", y);
    const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(d, "y ~ x", {.n_trees = 500, .max_depth = 1});
    // Whether each row is out of bag for each tree; the 500 trees are then grouped into
    // 50 forests of 10 and 100 forests of 5.
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> out(500);
    double left_out = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t t = 0; t < 500; ++t) {
      for (const int c : forest.in_bag_counts(t)) out[t].push_back(c == 0);
      left_out += std::accumulate(out[t].begin(), out[t].end(), 0.0) / (500.0 * n);
    }
    std::vector<double> counts10;
    double never = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
      for (std::size_t g = 0; g < 50; ++g) {
        int c = 0;
        for (std::size_t t = 10 * g; t < 10 * g + 10; ++t) c += out[t][i];
        counts10.push_back(c);
      }
      for (std::size_t g = 0; g < 100; ++g) {
        int c = 0;
        for (std::size_t t = 5 * g; t < 5 * g + 5; ++t) c += out[t][i];
        never += (c == 0) / (100.0 * n);
      }
    }
    double m = 0.0, v = 0.0;
    for (const double c : counts10) m += c / counts10.size();
    for (const double c : counts10) v += (c - m) * (c - m) / (counts10.size() - 1.0);
    const double qn = std::pow(1.0 - 1.0 / n, double(n));
    ns.push_back(n), frac.push_back(left_out), q.push_back(qn), mean_count.push_back(m), mean_th.push_back(10 * qn);
    var_count.push_back(v), var_th.push_back(10 * qn * (1 - qn)), never5.push_back(never), never5_th.push_back(std::pow(1 - qn, 5));
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("n", ns);
  table.add_column("share left out", frac);
  table.add_column("q_n", q);
  table.add_column("OOB trees of 10, mean", mean_count);
  table.add_column("10 q_n", mean_th);
  table.add_column("variance", var_count);
  table.add_column("10 q_n (1 - q_n)", var_th);
  table.add_column("never OOB in 5 trees", never5);
  table.add_column("(1 - q_n)^5", never5_th);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"n", 0}, {"OOB trees of 10, mean", 3}, {"10 q_n", 3}, {"variance", 3}, {"10 q_n (1 - q_n)", 3}};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | share left out | q_n | OOB trees of 10, mean | 10 q_n | variance | 10 q_n (1 - q_n) | never OOB in 5 trees | (1 - q_n)^5 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 10 | 0.3476 | 0.3487 | 3.476 | 3.487 | 2.202 | 2.271 | 0.1130 | 0.1172 |
| 20 | 0.3612 | 0.3585 | 3.612 | 3.585 | 2.426 | 2.300 | 0.1140 | 0.1087 |
| 50 | 0.3612 | 0.3642 | 3.612 | 3.642 | 2.213 | 2.316 | 0.1064 | 0.1039 |
| 150 | 0.3661 | 0.3666 | 3.661 | 3.666 | 2.260 | 2.322 | 0.0983 | 0.1019 |
| 1000 | 0.3678 | 0.3677 | 3.678 | 3.677 | 2.307 | 2.325 | 0.1000 | 0.1011 |
// result_end

\caption(Out-of-bag bookkeeping of forests of 500 trees on \(n\) rows, against the bootstrap probabilities.)

The share of rows a tree leaves out matches \(q_n\) to within 0.003, rising from 0.349 at \(n=10\) towards
\(e^{-1}=0.368\). The number of trees, out of 10, for which a row is out of bag has the binomial mean and
variance to within sampling error. With only 5 trees about one row in ten gets no out-of-bag vote
at all and is left out of the estimate; with 100 trees the chance is \(0.632^{100}\approx10^{-20}\).

>> The variance of the vote

The variance law \(\rho\sigma^2+(1-\rho)\sigma^2/B\) is checked on the noisy disc. For each of 1000 training sets of 200
points, a forest of 100 trees is grown, and the vote of every tree at the point \(x_0=(0.5,0.5)\) is
recorded (\(x_0\) is inside the disc, where class 1 has probability 0.85). The votes give \(\sigma^2\), the
variance of one tree's vote, and \(\rho\), the correlation between two trees of the same forest. The
variance over training sets of the vote share of the first \(B\) trees is then compared with the law,
for bagging (\(m=2\)) and for the random forest (\(m=1\)).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rf_variance.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const int reps = 1000, B = 100;
  const std::vector<int> sizes{1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100};
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> col_m, col_B, col_sim, col_law;
  const dm::RGB colours[2] = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}};
  for (const std::size_t m : {1, 2}) {
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> votes(reps, std::vector<double>(B));
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
      const auto train = dm::datasets::circle(200, 2, 0.15, 1000 + r);
      const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(train, "class ~ x1 + x2",
                                                     {.n_trees = B, .max_features = m, .seed = std::uint64_t(r + 1)});
      const auto v = forest.tree_votes({0.5, 0.5});
      for (int t = 0; t < B; ++t) votes[r][t] = forest.classes()[v[t]] == "1";
    }
    double mean = 0.0, var = 0.0, cov = 0.0;
    for (const auto& row : votes)
      for (const double v : row) mean += v / (reps * B);
    for (const auto& row : votes)
      for (const double v : row) var += (v - mean) * (v - mean) / (reps * B);
    for (const auto& row : votes)  // disjoint pairs of trees from the same forest
      for (int t = 0; t + 1 < B; t += 2) cov += (row[t] - mean) * (row[t + 1] - mean) / (reps * B / 2);
    const double rho = cov / var;
    std::print("m = {}: mean vote for class 1 {:.4f}, sigma^2 = {:.4f}, rho = {:.4f}, limit rho sigma^2 = {:.4f}\n\n",
               m, mean, var, rho, rho * var);
    std::vector<double> lx, sim, law;
    for (const int b : sizes) {
      double sm = 0.0, sv = 0.0;
      std::vector<double> share(reps);
      for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
        for (int t = 0; t < b; ++t) share[r] += votes[r][t] / b;
        sm += share[r] / reps;
      }
      for (const double s : share) sv += (s - sm) * (s - sm) / (reps - 1);
      lx.push_back(std::log10(b)), sim.push_back(sv), law.push_back(rho * var + (1 - rho) * var / b);
      col_m.push_back(m), col_B.push_back(b), col_sim.push_back(sv), col_law.push_back(law.back());
    }
    std::vector<double> gx, gy;
    for (double lb = 0.0; lb <= 2.0 + 1e-9; lb += 0.02)
      gx.push_back(lb), gy.push_back(rho * var + (1 - rho) * var / std::pow(10.0, lb));
    const auto c = colours[m - 1];
    fig.lines(gx, gy, std::format("m = {}, law", m), c, 2.0).points(lx, sim, std::format("m = {}, simulated", m), c, 7.0);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("m", col_m);
  table.add_column("B", col_B);
  table.add_column("variance, simulated", col_sim);
  table.add_column("rho sigma^2 + (1 - rho) sigma^2 / B", col_law);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"m", 0}, {"B", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));
  fig.x_ticks({0.0, std::log10(2.0), std::log10(5.0), 1.0, std::log10(20.0), std::log10(50.0), 2.0},
              {"1", "2", "5", "10", "20", "50", "100"})
      .x_limits(-0.05, 2.05).y_limits(0.0, 0.3).title("Variance of the vote share at (0.5, 0.5)")
      .x_label("number of trees B").y_label("variance over training sets");
  fig.size(800, 500);
  fig.save_svg("build/rf_variance.svg");
}
```
