\image(figures/rf_oob_path.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Mean over 200 training sets of the test error of the first \(t\) trees (black), of the out-of-bag error after \(t\) trees (blue), and of the test error at \(0.37t\) trees (red, from \(t\ge3\)); the grey line is the Bayes error 0.15. The axis for \(t\) is logarithmic.)
\alttext(Error against number of trees on a logarithmic axis from 1 to 200, with a grey line at 0.15. The black test-error curve starts at 0.30, rises to 0.31 at two trees, drops to 0.27 at three and falls smoothly to 0.23. The red curve, test error at 0.37 t trees, stays near 0.30 until five trees, then falls, above the black curve. The blue out-of-bag curve starts at 0.29, lies above the black curve and below the red one for small t, and meets the black curve near 50 trees, ending slightly below it at 0.225.)

With 200 trees the out-of-bag error is unbiased in practice: 0.2254 on average, against a test
error of 0.2294. The difference, \(-0.0039\), is less than two standard errors (0.0022). The
out-of-bag error of one training set varies twice as much as its test error (standard deviations
0.034 and 0.017), because it is measured on 200 noisy training labels rather than 5000 test
points. This is the spread seen in the disc example above.

With few trees the out-of-bag error is too high: 0.285 at \(t=5\) against a test error of 0.258. It
then follows the test error of a forest of \(0.37t\) trees, as predicted, to within 0.008 from \(t=10\)
on (0.264 against 0.272 at \(t=10\), 0.247 against 0.252 at 20). As the forest grows, a forest of
\(0.37t\) trees is as good as one of \(t\), and the three curves meet near the limit 0.23. The
out-of-bag error is reliable once each row has a few dozen out-of-bag votes, roughly \(B\ge50\). (The
test error of two trees is worse than that of one, 0.308 against 0.302: two trees tie whenever
they disagree, and ties go to class 1.) The limit, 0.23, is far above the Bayes error 0.15. Trees
grown to purity on 200 points with 15% flipped labels fit much of the noise, and averaging them
removes only part of it.

>> Strength and correlation against \(m\)

Breiman's twonorm benchmark (`dm::datasets::twonorm`) has 20 predictors and two Gaussian classes
whose means \(\pm(2/\sqrt{20})\mathbf 1\) are 4 apart, so the Bayes error is \(\Phi(-2)=0.0228\). Every predictor
carries the same weak signal: a tree has to combine many of them, which it does badly. For each \(m\),
five training sets of 300 rows, a forest of 300 trees each, and a test set of 3000 rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rf_strength.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto test = dm::datasets::twonorm(3000, 20, 99);
  const auto truth = test.get_column<std::string>("class").values();
  const int reps = 5;
  std::vector<double> ms, test_err, oob_err, tree_err, strength, corr, bound;
  for (const std::size_t m : {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 10, 15, 20}) {
    double te = 0, oe = 0, tr = 0, s = 0, rho = 0, b = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
      const auto train = dm::datasets::twonorm(300, 20, 100 + r);
      const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(train, "class ~ .", {.n_trees = 300, .max_features = m, .seed = std::uint64_t(r + 1)});
      const auto predicted = forest.predict(test);
      double wrong = 0;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < truth.size(); ++i) wrong += predicted[i] != truth[i];
      const auto sc = forest.strength_correlation();
      double tree_acc = 0;
      for (const double a : forest.oob_tree_accuracies()) tree_acc += a / forest.n_trees();
      te += wrong / truth.size() / reps, oe += (1 - forest.oob_accuracy()) / reps, tr += (1 - tree_acc) / reps;
      s += sc.strength / reps, rho += sc.correlation / reps, b += sc.error_bound / reps;
    }
    ms.push_back(m), test_err.push_back(te), oob_err.push_back(oe), tree_err.push_back(tr);
    strength.push_back(s), corr.push_back(rho), bound.push_back(b);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("m", ms);
  table.add_column("test error", test_err);
  table.add_column("out-of-bag error", oob_err);
  table.add_column("one tree (OOB)", tree_err);
  table.add_column("strength s", strength);
  table.add_column("correlation", corr);
  table.add_column("bound", bound);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"m", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));

  const dm::RGB ink{17, 24, 39}, blue{37, 99, 235}, red{220, 38, 38}, grey{148, 163, 184};
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.lines(ms, test_err, "test", ink, 2.0).points(ms, test_err, ink, 7.0)
      .lines(ms, oob_err, "out of bag", blue, 2.0).points(ms, oob_err, blue, 7.0)
      .lines({1.0, 20.0}, {0.02275, 0.02275}, "Bayes error", grey, 1.5);
  left.x_limits(0.0, 21.0).y_limits(0.0, 0.1).title("Forest error").x_label("predictors per split, m").y_label("error");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  right.lines(ms, strength, "strength s", blue, 2.0).points(ms, strength, blue, 7.0)
      .lines(ms, corr, "correlation", red, 2.0).points(ms, corr, red, 7.0);
  right.x_limits(0.0, 21.0).y_limits(0.0, 0.75).title("Strength and correlation").x_label("predictors per split, m").y_label("out-of-bag estimate");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/rf_strength.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| m | test error | out-of-bag error | one tree (OOB) | strength s | correlation | bound |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.0347 | 0.0287 | 0.2387 | 0.5223 | 0.0875 | 0.2337 |
| 2 | 0.0373 | 0.0313 | 0.2279 | 0.5439 | 0.0983 | 0.2350 |
| 3 | 0.0401 | 0.0333 | 0.2251 | 0.5494 | 0.1042 | 0.2415 |
| 4 | 0.0430 | 0.0473 | 0.2262 | 0.5475 | 0.1114 | 0.2614 |
| 6 | 0.0493 | 0.0453 | 0.2260 | 0.5477 | 0.1171 | 0.2734 |
| 10 | 0.0579 | 0.0487 | 0.2251 | 0.5494 | 0.1269 | 0.2939 |
| 15 | 0.0673 | 0.0580 | 0.2248 | 0.5502 | 0.1426 | 0.3272 |
| 20 | 0.0753 | 0.0667 | 0.2254 | 0.5491 | 0.1491 | 0.3442 |


\image(figures/rf_strength.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Twonorm with 20 predictors, 300 training rows, 300 trees, mean of five training sets. Left: test error (black) and out-of-bag error (blue) against the number \(m\) of predictors tried at each split; the grey line is the Bayes error. Right: the out-of-bag estimates of Breiman's strength \(s\) (blue) and mean correlation \(\bar\rho\) (red).)
\alttext(Left: error against m from 1 to 20. The black test-error curve rises steadily from 0.035 at m equals 1 to 0.075 at m equals 20; the blue out-of-bag curve runs a little below it, from 0.029 to 0.067, crossing it once at m equals 4. A grey line marks the Bayes error at 0.023. Right: the strength stays flat near 0.55 for all m, a little lower at m equals 1 (0.52); the correlation rises slowly from 0.09 to 0.15.)

A single tree is weak on twonorm, with out-of-bag error 0.22 to 0.24 whatever \(m\) is, ten times the
Bayes error. The forest of 300 such trees errs on 3.5% of test points with \(m=1\), within 1.2 points
of the Bayes error, because weak but nearly independent trees average well. The bagged forest
(\(m=20\)) errs on 7.5%, twice as often. The out-of-bag estimates show why. The strength hardly
changes (0.52 at \(m=1\), 0.54 to 0.55 for every larger \(m\)), while the correlation between trees rises
steadily from 0.088 to 0.149. With every predictor equally useful, letting a split choose among
more of them makes the trees more alike without making them stronger. Breiman's bound, 0.23 to
0.34, is five to seven times the test error, so it is far from tight. But it moves with the error
and for the same reason: \(\bar\rho\) grows while \(s\) does not. The out-of-bag errors track the test errors
to within 0.01.

> Fisher's iris data

`dm::datasets::iris()` holds Fisher's (1936) 150 flowers, 50 of each of the species *setosa*,
*versicolor* and *virginica*, measured on sepal length and width and petal length and width (cm).
*Setosa* is separated from the others by either petal measurement; *versicolor* and *virginica*
overlap slightly.

>> The forest and its out-of-bag error

A forest of 500 trees with the defaults: \(m=\lfloor\sqrt4\rfloor=2\), Gini splits, depth at most 10.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rf_iris_oob.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(iris, "Species ~ .", {.n_trees = 500});
  std::print("```\n{}```\n\n", forest.summary());
  const auto sc = forest.strength_correlation();
  std::print("Strength {:.4f}, correlation {:.4f}, bound {:.4f}.\n\n", sc.strength, sc.correlation, sc.error_bound);

  const auto margins = forest.oob_margins();
  const auto share = forest.oob_vote_share();
  const auto oob = forest.oob_predictions();
  std::vector<double> row, mg, s0, s1, s2;
  std::vector<std::string> species, predicted;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < margins.size(); ++i) {
    if (margins[i] >= 0.2) continue;
    row.push_back(i + 1), species.push_back(iris.string_at("Species", i)), predicted.push_back(oob[i]);
    s0.push_back(share[i][0]), s1.push_back(share[i][1]), s2.push_back(share[i][2]), mg.push_back(margins[i]);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("row", row);
  table.add_column("species", species);
  table.add_column("OOB prediction", predicted);
  table.add_column("votes setosa", s0);
  table.add_column("votes versicolor", s1);
  table.add_column("votes virginica", s2);
  table.add_column("margin", mg);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 3;
  md.column_digits = {{"row", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));

  const auto path = forest.oob_accuracy_path();
  std::vector<double> t(path.size());
  std::iota(t.begin(), t.end(), 1.0);
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.lines(t, path, "out-of-bag accuracy", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0);
  left.x_limits(0.0, 500.0).y_limits(0.9, 1.0).title("Out-of-bag accuracy").x_label("trees").y_label("accuracy");
  // Margins sorted within species.
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const dm::RGB colours[3] = {{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {220, 38, 38}};
  double offset = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c) {
    std::vector<double> ys;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < margins.size(); ++i)
      if (iris.string_at("Species", i) == forest.classes()[c]) ys.push_back(margins[i]);
    std::sort(ys.begin(), ys.end());
    std::vector<double> xs(ys.size());
    std::iota(xs.begin(), xs.end(), offset + 1.0);
    right.points(xs, ys, forest.classes()[c], colours[c], 5.0);
    offset += ys.size();
  }
  right.lines({0.0, 151.0}, {0.0, 0.0}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0);
  right.x_limits(0.0, 151.0).y_limits(-1.05, 1.5).title("Out-of-bag margins").x_label("flower, sorted within species").y_label("margin");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/rf_iris_oob.svg");
}
```
