\caption(Left: out-of-bag accuracy of the iris forest after each of its 500 trees. Right: the out-of-bag margin of every flower, sorted within species (*setosa* blue, *versicolor* green, *virginica* red); a flower below the grey line is misclassified out of bag.)
\alttext(Left: out-of-bag accuracy against trees from 1 to 500, between 0.93 and 0.96 in the first 20 trees, then stepping between 0.947 and 0.96, ending at 0.953. Right: margins of 150 flowers sorted within species. The 50 setosa margins are all between 0.86 and 1. The versicolor margins rise from three values near minus 0.8 to minus 0.6, one at 0.16, then 0.56 up to 1. The virginica margins rise from minus 0.97 and minus 0.59, through minus 0.24, 0, 0.08 and 0.09, to 1.)

The training accuracy is 100%, which says nothing: every deep tree fits its own sample, and each
training flower was in the sample of about 63% of the trees. The out-of-bag accuracy, 95.33%, is
143 of 150 flowers. It settles after about 25 trees and then moves by one flower (0.0067) at a
time. Every *setosa* has a margin of at least 0.86. Seven flowers have a margin of zero or below.
Three *versicolor* (rows 71, 78, 84) get 80% to 92% of their out-of-bag votes for *virginica*, and
three *virginica* (107, 120, 134) are voted *versicolor*. Row 135 is an exact tie, 0.5 against 0.5,
broken towards *versicolor*. The strength is 0.871 and the correlation 0.593. With four predictors
and \(m=2\), the trees often get both petal measurements and are much alike. Breiman's bound, 0.189,
is four times the out-of-bag error 0.047.

*Against scikit-learn.* Over 50 seeds, this forest's out-of-bag accuracy is \(0.9543\pm0.0052\) (mean and
standard deviation). scikit-learn's `RandomForestClassifier` with the same settings (500 trees,
\(\sqrt p\) predictors per split, depth 10) gives \(0.9553\pm0.0047\). Both misclassify rows 71, 78, 84, 107,
120 and 134 for every seed and differ only in how often they miss the borderline rows 130, 135 and
139. For single trees the agreement is exact. The full-depth tree on iris and on the wine data has
the same importances to \(10^{-12}\) as scikit-learn's `DecisionTreeClassifier` for some of its random
orders of trying the predictors (which break ties between equally good splits). It also has the
same number of nodes on iris, wine and breast cancer (17, 23 and 43).

>> Which measurements matter

To see the difference between the two importances, two predictors unrelated to the species are
added: `noise`, drawn from \(N(0,1)\), and `coin`, a fair 0/1 coin.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rf_iris_importance.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(4);
  std::vector<double> noise(150), coin(150);
  for (int i = 0; i < 150; ++i) noise[i] = rng.normal(0.0, 1.0), coin[i] = rng.uniform(0.0, 1.0) < 0.5;
  iris.add_column("noise", noise);
  iris.add_column("coin", coin);
  const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(iris, "Species ~ .", {.n_trees = 500});
  const auto mdi = forest.feature_importance();
  const auto perm = forest.permutation_importance();
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("predictor", perm.predictor_names);
  table.add_column("mean decrease in impurity", mdi);
  table.add_column("permutation importance", perm.importance);
  table.add_column("standard error", perm.standard_error);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  std::print("{}\n\nOut-of-bag accuracy with the two extra predictors: {:.4f}.\n\n", table.to_md(md), forest.oob_accuracy());

  std::vector<double> at{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"S.Len", "S.Wid", "P.Len", "P.Wid", "noise", "coin"};
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.bars(at, mdi, "", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, 0.7);
  left.x_ticks(at, labels).x_limits(0.4, 6.6).y_limits(0.0, 0.5).title("Mean decrease in impurity").x_label("predictor").y_label("share");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  right.bars(at, perm.importance, "", dm::RGB{253, 186, 116}, 0.7);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j)
    right.segments({at[j]}, {perm.importance[j] - 2 * perm.standard_error[j]}, {at[j]},
                   {perm.importance[j] + 2 * perm.standard_error[j]}, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5);
  right.lines({0.4, 6.6}, {0.0, 0.0}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0);
  right.x_ticks(at, labels).x_limits(0.4, 6.6).y_limits(-0.02, 0.4).title("Permutation importance").x_label("predictor").y_label("drop in accuracy");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/rf_iris_importance.svg");
}
