\image(figures/rf_iris_importance.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Importance of the four iris measurements and two pure-noise predictors in a forest of 500 trees. Left: mean decrease in Gini impurity, normalised to sum to 1. Right: out-of-bag permutation importance, the mean drop in a tree's out-of-bag accuracy when the predictor is shuffled, with \(\pm2\) standard errors (black).)
\alttext(Two bar charts over six predictors. Left, mean decrease in impurity: petal length 0.40 and petal width 0.38, sepal length 0.13, sepal width 0.06, noise 0.03 and coin almost 0. Right, permutation importance: petal length 0.29 and petal width 0.27 with short error bars, sepal length 0.06, sepal width 0.02, and no visible bar for noise or coin.)

Both importances rank the petal measurements first and sepal length third, but they disagree on
the noise. The impurity importance gives `noise` 3.1% of the credit, about half of what it gives
sepal width. That is the bias derived above: deep in a tree, a continuous predictor always offers
some threshold that separates a few remaining rows. `coin` offers a single threshold and gets
only 0.4%. The permutation importance of both is zero within its standard error (\(0.0001\pm0.0009\) and
\(0.0000\pm0.0005\)), while all four measurements are many standard errors above zero. Shuffling petal
length costs a tree 29 points of out-of-bag accuracy.

The extra predictors also change the impurity shares of the real ones. Sepal length goes from
0.098 without them to 0.127 with them. With six predictors and \(m=2\), a node is offered neither
petal measurement with probability \(\binom42/\binom62=0.4\), against \(1/\binom42=0.17\) with four, so the trees
split on sepal measurements and noise more often. The out-of-bag accuracy is unchanged at 95.33%.

>> A forest against a single tree

Out-of-bag accuracy is one estimate; held-out data give another. Over 200 random stratified splits
into 100 training and 50 test flowers, the forest and a single fully grown tree are fit to the
training flowers and scored on the test flowers. The figure compares the two in the plane of the
petal measurements.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rf_iris_regions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  const int reps = 200;
  std::vector<double> forest_acc, tree_acc, oob_acc;
  for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
    const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(iris, 1.0 / 3.0, r + 1, "Species");
    const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier forest(train, "Species ~ .", {.n_trees = 500, .seed = std::uint64_t(r + 1)});
    const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier tree(train, "Species ~ .", {.max_depth = 10});
    const auto y = test.get_column<std::string>("Species").values();
    const auto pf = forest.predict(test), pt = tree.predict(test);
    double a = 0, b = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < y.size(); ++i) a += pf[i] == y[i], b += pt[i] == y[i];
    forest_acc.push_back(a / y.size()), tree_acc.push_back(b / y.size()), oob_acc.push_back(forest.oob_accuracy());
  }
  auto mean = [](const std::vector<double>& v) { return std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0.0) / v.size(); };
  auto sd = [&](const std::vector<double>& v) {
    double m = mean(v), s = 0;
    for (const double x : v) s += (x - m) * (x - m) / (v.size() - 1);
    return std::sqrt(s);
  };
  std::vector<double> diff(reps);
  for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) diff[r] = forest_acc[r] - tree_acc[r];
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("estimate", std::vector<std::string>{"forest, test flowers", "forest, out of bag (training flowers)",
                                                        "single tree, test flowers", "forest minus tree, test flowers"});
  table.add_column("mean accuracy", std::vector<double>{mean(forest_acc), mean(oob_acc), mean(tree_acc), mean(diff)});
  table.add_column("SD over splits", std::vector<double>{sd(forest_acc), sd(oob_acc), sd(tree_acc), sd(diff)});
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  std::print("{}\n\nForest better on {} splits, tree better on {}, tied on {}.\n\n", table.to_md(md),
             std::count_if(diff.begin(), diff.end(), [](double d) { return d > 0; }),
             std::count_if(diff.begin(), diff.end(), [](double d) { return d < 0; }),
             std::count_if(diff.begin(), diff.end(), [](double d) { return d == 0; }));

  const std::string f = "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width";
  const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier one(iris, f, {.n_trees = 1, .max_depth = 10, .max_features = 2, .bootstrap = false});
  const dm::stats::RandomForestClassifier many(iris, f, {.n_trees = 500});
  std::print("Petal measurements only: forest out-of-bag accuracy {:.4f}.\n", many.oob_accuracy());
  const std::array<double, 4> box{0.7, 7.2, 0.0, 3.2};
  auto a = one.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", 200, box);
  auto b = many.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", 200, box);
  a.title("One tree");
  b.title("Random forest, 500 trees");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(a);
  fig.add(b);
  fig.size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/rf_iris_regions.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| estimate | mean accuracy | SD over splits |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| forest, test flowers | 0.9504 | 0.0232 |
| forest, out of bag (training flowers) | 0.9512 | 0.0154 |
| single tree, test flowers | 0.9425 | 0.0246 |
| forest minus tree, test flowers | 0.0078 | 0.0188 |


Forest better on 75 splits, tree better on 23, tied on 102.

Petal measurements only: forest out-of-bag accuracy 0.9467.
\image(figures/rf_iris_regions.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The species predicted from the petal measurements by one tree grown on all 150 flowers (left) and by a forest of 500 trees (right): *setosa* blue, *versicolor* red, *virginica* green. The forest's shading is darkest where at least 90% of the trees agree, paler for 60% to 90%, palest below 60%.)
\alttext(Two maps of petal width against petal length. Left, one tree: solid rectangles, blue for petal length below 2.45, red for length 2.45 to 4.95 with width below 1.65, green elsewhere, with a small red notch just right of length 4.95 near width 1.6. Right, the forest: the same three regions with dark cores around the flowers of each species, a pale band along the boundary between red and green near length 4.8 to 5.2 and width 1.5 to 1.8, and pale regions far from any flowers, such as long petals with small widths and short petals with large widths.)

On held-out flowers the forest is right 95.0% of the time, the single tree 94.3%. The forest
wins on 75 splits, loses on 23 and ties on 102. Its advantage, 0.78 points, is small but about
six standard errors (0.0188 over \(\sqrt{200}\)). The out-of-bag accuracy computed on the 100 training
flowers of each split averages 95.1%, within 0.1 point of the forest's held-out accuracy. It
also varies less across splits (standard deviation 0.015 against 0.023) because it uses 100
flowers rather than 50. On iris, then, the out-of-bag estimate does the job of a test set.

With the petal measurements alone, the single tree draws three rectangles and a notch, each
boundary placed by one cut. The forest keeps the same layout but shows where the trees
disagree. The band between *versicolor* and *virginica* near petal length 5 is pale. So are
regions far from any flower, where the trees' cuts fall anywhere between the clusters. Its
out-of-bag accuracy with the two petal measurements is 94.7%.

> Conclusion

A random forest reduces the variance of deep trees by averaging them, and the averaging is only as
good as the trees are different. The variance of the vote falls as \(\rho\sigma^2+(1-\rho)\sigma^2/B\). Trees beyond a
few dozen change little, and the floor \(\rho\sigma^2\) is set by the correlation between trees. Random
predictor subsets lower that correlation: by a fifth on the noisy disc, and enough on twonorm to
halve the error relative to bagging, while the trees' strength hardly changed. The bootstrap
gives a test set for free. About \(e^{-1}\) of the rows are out of bag for each tree, and the
out-of-bag error matched the test error once the forest had about 50 trees, both on simulated data
and on iris (95.1% against 95.0%).

The method has limits that the examples showed. Deep trees fit label noise, and averaging removes
only part of it: on the noisy disc the forest's error stayed at 0.21, against a Bayes error of
0.15. A forest also gives up the readability of a single tree. Its importances need care: the
impurity importance credited a pure-noise predictor with 3% of the splits, and only the
permutation importance, measured out of bag, put it at zero. On a problem as easy as iris, the
forest beats a single tree by less than one point.

